add(113,'C The finite nonnegative-sum inequality explicitly includes an empty competitor family, with both sides zero.|C Zero Bhattacharyya coefficients contribute zero for positive 1/rho; zero optimal errors give infinite exponents.|N R>0 means the target M_n is at least two; the theorem has no R=0 reliability assertion.|C Deterministic channels and zero-error subcodes are admitted by the same expurgation inequality.|C Rho=1 is included by the equality branch r=1; every rho is finite before optimizing the supremum.|C A finite random codebook realization and a finite set of good messages provide the expurgated code.|N This is a lower bound on both liminf error exponents, not equality with the general reliability function.')
add(114,'N Nonempty channel alphabets and finite codebook supports supply deterministic realizations.|C Rho=0 is restored by the zero-limit argument in step 4.1; zero transitions are identically zero near rho=0.|N R>0 excludes the one-message and rate-zero reliability branches.|C Zero dispersion or identical rows do not invalidate the bound; positivity E_r>0 is claimed only for 0<R<C.|C Rho=1 is covered by affine power Jensen; R=C has no strict positivity claim.|C Fixed P,rho are optimized only after obtaining each finite-code bound; finite expurgation provides maximal error.|N Both conclusions are achievability inequalities, expressly not exact reliability formulas.')
add(115,'N Allowed auxiliary channels form a nonempty compact product because every row of W has nonempty support.|C Zero channel entries are forbidden in V support; zero P terms are omitted and R_infinity>=0 in step 1.1.|N R>R_infinity>=0 implies R>0 and eventual type subcode size at least two.|C The separating weight v cannot vanish because a strict-feasible V0 exists; this is proved in step 3.2.|C R=R_infinity is explicitly excluded, and strict feasibility is used to pass from open to closed constrained minima.|C AC and Countable Choice are explicitly required by the weak-separation supplier [A1,F8].|N This is an upper error-exponent bound under strict feasibility, not an unconditional or biconditional statement.')
add(116,'N Nonempty CAID set and finite code alphabets guarantee a minimum-dispersion law and at least one code.|N V_min=0 and epsilon=0 are explicitly excluded; zero transition entries are still admitted on their charged supports.|C One-message codes provide a nonempty size class, while asymptotic achievability has M>=2 because V_min>0 implies C>0 in step 3.2.|N Zero dispersion is outside the theorem; degenerate inactive input coordinates remain allowed in nearby types.|C The fixed interval 0<epsilon<1/2 makes z_epsilon<0; epsilon=1/2 and above are explicitly unclaimed.|C AC is explicit for normals and Berry–Esseen; finite type selection and expurgation use determined subsets.|N The theorem gives two normal expansions under stated restrictions, not an iff or a third-order coefficient.')
add(117,'N Finite code spaces have nonempty minimizing-code sets for each n.|C The conclusion is that all four error exponents are zero, using optimal error probabilities tending to one.|N R>C>=0 means R>0 and M_n>=2; no one-message endpoint is asserted.|C Zero-capacity channels are allowed: every positive R is above capacity and has zero error exponent.|C Strict R>C is essential; R=C is outside this theorem.|C Each finite minimum is attained; strong-converse bounds apply to any chosen minimizing sequence without extra arbitrary choices.|N The theorem proves above-capacity zero exponents; it does not claim a converse characterization of all zero-exponent rates.')
